-      <p class="why-it-matters">An instrument is a claim with terms, and this lesson sorts each trade by one of two ways of setting those terms. Some trades run on a venue that fixes the rules for the instruments it lists. Others are negotiated directly between two parties. Before a student can classify an instrument or follow a trade, the classification has to be exact. This lesson defines the two kinds of market, sets out a three-axis taxonomy of instruments, and proves the counting rule that makes the taxonomy usable in a register, a list of entries defined in Definition 2.1 (counting rule: Proposition 2.5). The register in the lab is typed in and describes no real market.</p>
+      <p class="why-it-matters">An instrument is a claim with terms, and this lesson sorts each trade by one of two ways of setting those terms. Some trades run on a venue that fixes the rules for the instruments it lists. Others are negotiated directly between two parties. Before a student can classify an instrument or follow a trade, the classification has to be exact. This lesson defines the two kinds of trade, sets out a three-axis taxonomy of instruments, and proves the counting rule that makes the taxonomy usable in a register, a list of entries defined in Definition 2.1 (counting rule: Proposition 2.5). The register in the lab is typed in and describes no real market.</p>
