-      <p class="why-it-matters">In the model below, a dealer earns the spread on each share it trades and holds the shares it has not yet passed on. These are the two terms of Proposition 2.3 in Section 2. This lesson defines the dealer and the bid–ask spread, then proves an exact identity: the dealer's profit valued at the mid (Definition 1.2; mark-to-mid is Definition 2.1) equals half the quoted spread times the shares traded, plus a term that comes from mid-price moves. A two-dealer game with one-cent prices shows that the equilibrium ask can sit 0, 1 or 2 cents above cost. Lab 1 checks the two identities in whole cents and enumerates the grid of the tick game.</p>
+      <p class="why-it-matters">In the model below, a dealer earns half the quoted spread on each share it trades, against that trade's mid, and the mid can move between trades. These are the two terms of Proposition 2.3 in Section 2. This lesson defines the dealer and the bid–ask spread, then proves an identity: the dealer's profit valued at the mid (Definition 1.2; mark-to-mid is Definition 2.1) equals half the quoted spread times the shares traded, plus a term that comes from mid-price moves. A two-dealer game with one-cent prices shows that the equilibrium ask (defined in Section 3) can sit 0, 1 or 2 cents above cost. Lab 1 checks both identities in whole cents and enumerates the tick-game grid.</p>
